  "image_name": "1e655827819f8ea3c44687e8f8ef8348.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several ways CDK4/6 inhibition can affect cancer cells and the tumour environment, showing that its consequences extend beyond the classic RB–E2F pathway. A central theme is that blocking Cyclin D–CDK4/6 prevents RB inactivation, thereby restraining E2F-driven programs and causing loss of proliferative signals. This is linked to outcomes such as proliferative arrest, DNA damage responses, chromatin remodeling, metabolic changes, differentiation, and sometimes apoptosis.\n\nThe diagram also emphasizes broader effects. In senescence, CDK4/6 inhibition appears to cooperate with additional targets and p53-related pathways to drive cell-cycle withdrawal, chromatin changes, metabolic dysregulation, apoptosis resistance, and the SASP. In apoptosis and cytostasis, the figure contrasts solid tumours, where CDK4–Cyclin D1 acts mainly through RB and E2F to produce cytostasis, with haematological malignancies, where CDK6–Cyclin D3 seems to regulate metabolic enzymes, altered metabolism, and increased ROS, leading more directly to apoptosis.\n\nOther panels highlight non-canonical RB functions, including recruitment of histone modifiers and activation of other transcription factors, as well as non-RB substrates unique to CDK4 or CDK6. Finally, the figure suggests these drugs can influence lymphocytes, fibroblasts, and endothelial cells, shaping overall clinical response.",
